╭────────────────────────────────────────── Full verifier detail — MQTT client for publishing telemetry to a broker ──────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Reasoning: The connector's core objection -- that gmqtt needs a persistent event loop for its whole lifecycle while TelemetryBridge is synchronous -- is    │
│ exactly what the wrapper attempts to solve via a dedicated background thread running loop.run_forever() plus run_coroutine_threadsafe for                   │
│ connect()/disconnect(). That pattern is a legitimate, well-established way to bridge asyncio-only clients into sync call sites, so this is not a structural │
│ dead end. However, the wrapper's publish() calls self._client.publish(...) directly from the caller's thread instead of marshaling it onto the background   │
│ event-loop thread. gmqtt's Client.publish() touches the asyncio Transport/StreamWriter bound to the loop running in the other thread; invoking it from a    │
│ foreign thread is not safe (asyncio transports/protocols are not thread-safe outside their owning loop) and can corrupt in-flight packets or raise          │
│ intermittent errors under concurrent publish calls, even though it happens to work in a single-threaded smoke test that never actually publishes over a     │
│ real connection.                                                                                                                                            │
│                                                                                                                                                             │
│ Required repair: In _SyncMQTTClient.publish(), do not call self._client.publish(topic, payload, qos=qos) directly from the caller's thread. Instead marshal │
│ it onto the event loop thread, e.g. self._loop.call_soon_threadsafe(self._client.publish, topic, payload, qos) (or wrap it in a trivial coroutine and drive │
│ it through self._run(...)), so all interaction with gmqtt's transport happens on the thread that owns the event loop.                                       │
